import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,a3 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BLaSdaBb.js";const o="/screens/holdings-hub.png",n="/screens/city-jobs.png",i="/screens/city-works.png",w=JSON.parse('{"title":"Cities & Holdings","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/cities.md","filePath":"guide/cities.md"}'),h={name:"guide/cities.md"};function l(d,e,g,c,u,p){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="cities-holdings" tabindex="-1">Cities &amp; Holdings <a class="header-anchor" href="#cities-holdings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Cities &amp; Holdings&quot;">​</a></h1><p><img src="'+o+'" alt="The Holdings hub"></p><p><em>Left side shows tabs for that holding&#39;s features (Jobs, Works, Garrison, etc.). Right side shows the content. Use ◂ ▸ to jump between your holdings without closing the hub.</em></p><p>Tap any holding on the map, yours or not, and the <strong>Holdings hub</strong> opens on it. If one of your own columns is standing there, the tile&#39;s <a href="/guide/heroes.html#a-holdings-callout">muster roll</a> comes up first. The holding&#39;s own seal carries the four doors you reach for most: Recruit, Reinforce, Works and Jobs. <strong>Open ›</strong> goes through to the hub itself.</p><p>The hub is one board for the whole realm. It offers only the sections that holding actually has, so a city shows Jobs, Works, Garrison and its drill yard, while a gold mine shows what it is and what it pays. <strong>◂ ▸</strong> in the header walks every holding you own without closing anything, and 🏛 on the top bar opens the hub on the list of them all.</p><p>Cities level from 1 to 3. Each level adds job slots, a floor to the skyline, and eventually unlocks the best projects. A <strong>village</strong> you hold can be raised into one: from its Overview, 400🪙, 40💎 and a Follower to take the reeve&#39;s chair make it a level-1 town with crafts, a timber palisade and a yard that musters Warriors. It keeps the name it grew up with.</p><h2 id="jobs" tabindex="-1">Jobs <a class="header-anchor" href="#jobs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Jobs&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="The jobs bench"></p><p><em>Assign Followers to crafts: each one produces a resource daily. The numbers next to each craft show how many Followers are assigned (and thus the craft level). Use + and − to pencil in assignments, then Save.</em></p><p>Spare <strong>Followers</strong> work the city&#39;s crafts: the <strong>Market</strong> (gold), <strong>Tavern</strong> (elixir), <strong>Temple</strong> (reagents), the <strong>Magistrate</strong> (infamy), and the <strong>Bestiary</strong> (beast lore) once you have raised one.</p><p><strong>Bread is not among them.</strong> Food is grown in the fields, not levied from a city desk. See <em>New Fields</em> below.</p><p>Every craft pays the same daily <strong>worth</strong>; only the coin differs. One keeper earns <strong>25 gold</strong>, or <strong>16 elixir</strong>, or <strong>12 reagents</strong>. That is the same value at the rates guilds trade them, so posting a keeper is a choice of <em>what</em> your city makes, never of how much. The Magistrate is the exception: infamy is not traded, and it earns at its own slow pace. Infamy still matters. Your deeds plus your magistrates decide how many new Followers arrive every seventh day.</p><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Followers are permanently assigned</p><p>When you post a Follower to a craft, they stay there. You can&#39;t move them later. The number of Followers you post sets that craft&#39;s level. Some features (like the Bestiary) only unlock at higher craft levels.</p></div><p>Because it is permanent, the panel lets you <strong>pencil in</strong> as many as you like first. <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> draw and rub out pips across every craft, and nothing leaves the muster until you press <strong>Save</strong>. The warning comes once, about the whole batch. <strong>Clear</strong> throws the lot away.</p><h2 id="new-fields" tabindex="-1">New Fields <a class="header-anchor" href="#new-fields" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;New Fields&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A city grows a small crop of its own and no more, enough for a handful of mouths. Everything past that has to be <strong>sown</strong>.</p><p>From the <strong>Works</strong> bench, <strong>New Fields</strong> breaks open ground you hold within a few tiles of the walls: <strong>150🪙</strong>, and it feeds you <strong>12🍞 a day</strong> from tomorrow. Raise as many as you have ground for.</p><p>This is the shape of the whole game&#39;s growth. Every recruit costs <strong>bread</strong>, so fields are what an army is made of, and an army is what takes the ground the next field goes on. A treasury with nothing to spend itself on is a treasury that should be buying furrows.</p><p>A <strong>Charter of the Furrow</strong>, found as loot, still sows a field for free wherever a party is standing on open ground you hold.</p><h2 id="walls-and-garrisons" tabindex="-1">Walls and garrisons <a class="header-anchor" href="#walls-and-garrisons" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Walls and garrisons&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Any holding takes at most <strong>10 soldiers</strong>: enough to hold a gate, not enough to make a fortress unassailable.</p><p>Defenders fight a little steadier for standing behind stone. A city or Grand Athanor is worth something on its own, and a city&#39;s <strong>walls</strong> add their course on top. They also sharpen the drill of everyone posted behind them, in peacetime and during a defence.</p><p><strong>Walls belong to the city, not to a tile.</strong> Every city is founded as a walled town, and raising the walls builds the city itself up rather than putting a separate structure on the ground beside it: open the <strong>Build</strong> bench and pick <strong>City Walls</strong>. Each course is paid for at once and shows on the city&#39;s miniature as taller towers, with pennants at the third course. Walls cannot outgrow the Town Hall, so raise the hall to raise the walls further.</p><h2 id="food-farms" tabindex="-1">Food &amp; farms <a class="header-anchor" href="#food-farms" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Food &amp; farms&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The faithful march on bread, not coin. Every <strong>recruit costs 1 Follower, 40 food and 20 elixir</strong>; gold buys mercenaries, structures and training, never the faithful. Cities grow a base crop (villages a smaller one), Farm workers grow more, and for real granaries you settle <strong>farmsteads</strong>.</p><p>New ground is broken with a <strong>Charter of the Furrow</strong>, seed-stock found in a Marauder hold or off the battlefield&#39;s fallen. It is never bought. Carry one in a party&#39;s satchel, march onto held, empty ground, and <strong>Settle a farmstead</strong> appears in that party&#39;s <strong>Skills</strong> sheet. Whoever leads presents the charter, and it is spent doing so. One charter, one field.</p><p>So where your bread grows is a question of where your columns have been. Clear the Marauders out of a hold and search it: a charter is worth as much to a guild as the sword beside it.</p><p>Each farmstead yields 10 food a day at full control, and like any holding of yours its fences <strong>ward the blight</strong>. A ring of farms is both a food supply and a wall.</p><h2 id="hiring" tabindex="-1">Hiring <a class="header-anchor" href="#hiring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Hiring&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Recruit</strong> sits in the Army hub, and behind the ⚔ slot on the command card. It is the one door onto everything a holding will take on, and it works like the Works bench: the list names what is on offer here, and tapping a row explains what it costs to keep before you spend a coin on it. Its other half, <em>Muster from afar</em>, raises a recruit at a holding somewhere else and walks them to your army.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Hire</th><th>Where</th><th>Costs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Follower</strong></td><td>any hall that trains that calling</td><td>1👥 + 40🍞 + 20⚗️</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Mercenary</strong></td><td>the same</td><td>120🪙, no Follower</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Beastmaster</strong></td><td>a level-3 city</td><td>200🪙 + 1👥</td></tr><tr><td><strong>A beast</strong></td><td>its own captured lair</td><td>a fortune, and daily gold</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A veteran of the same calling standing at the hall is a <strong>mentor</strong>: a new recruit starts at half their level. Mercenaries never get it.</p><h2 id="the-drill-yard" tabindex="-1">The drill yard <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-drill-yard" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The drill yard&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Every holding that drills a calling has a <strong>Drill</strong> section. Every unit standing there, in a party or the garrison, can buy its next level for gold and elixir. The drillmaster only charges for what is left to learn: a soldier already three-quarters of the way to the next level pays a quarter of the list price, so battle scars are money in the bank. Training takes the day; the queue shows who&#39;s drilling. Keep a high-level veteran garrisoned and new recruits start at half their level.</p><p>Barracks train warriors, Laboratories train alchemists, Shadow Guilds train rogues; cities and Grand Athanors drill warriors. Tap any owned trainer building on the map to open its yard directly. A soldier your ground cannot drill can be <strong>sent to the nearest hall that can</strong> (see <a href="/guide/heroes.html">Heroes &amp; Units</a>).</p><h2 id="works" tabindex="-1">Works <a class="header-anchor" href="#works" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Works&quot;">​</a></h2><p><img src="'+i+'" alt="The works bench"></p><p><em>Manage both building and research: pick the Build or Research tab at top, then choose what to work on. Shows what&#39;s in progress (top strip) and what projects are available.</em></p><p>Raising stone and studying arts share one bench, behind two tabs: <strong>Build</strong> and <strong>Research</strong>. Each tab carries only its own filters, on the row beneath. Stone is cut down to <em>All</em>, <em>To raise</em> or <em>Raised</em>; arts to a calling. What the realm has in hand shows above the panel, wherever it is being raised or studied.</p><h3 id="build" tabindex="-1">Build <a class="header-anchor" href="#build" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Cities can raise new structures on free land beside them: a Barracks, Laboratory or Distillery, and at city level 3 a Shadow Guild. One project at a time, a few days each, and <strong>one of each per city</strong>. Once a structure stands, its row becomes the <strong>upgrade</strong> path instead. A structure can rise <strong>up to its city&#39;s level</strong> (raise the city first to raise its halls higher). Each level multiplies the structure&#39;s daily yield, and each level above the first cuts training costs at that hall by 15%. Tap the structure on the map to see exactly what an upgrade buys and costs.</p><p>The same bench also carries <strong>City Walls</strong>, which are not a structure at all. They are the city&#39;s own curtain, so raising them takes no land and no days. See <a href="#walls-and-garrisons">Walls and garrisons</a>.</p><h3 id="research" tabindex="-1">Research <a class="header-anchor" href="#research" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Research&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The Town Hall researches hero skills, one project at a time, guild-wide once learned. Nothing is granted. Even a Lord&#39;s own class art (<em>Rally</em>, <em>Ambush</em>, <em>Transmute Blast</em>) is a study: the cheapest on the bench, and the one to start with. A <strong>level‑2 city</strong> unlocks the deeper studies (<em>Shield Wall</em>, <em>Plunder</em>, <em>Field Mending</em>) and a <strong>level‑3 city</strong> the masterworks (<em>Warcry</em>, <em>Overwhelm</em>, <em>Quicksilver March</em>). Once researched, assign the skill on any matching hero&#39;s sheet. The row of pills beneath the tab cuts thirty arts down to one calling, or to just what can go on the bench today.</p><p>The Town Hall is not the only school. A <strong>Barracks</strong>, <strong>Laboratory</strong>, <strong>Shadow Guild</strong> or <strong>Bestiary</strong> teaches arts of its own trade that the hall never offers, which is a reason to raise them past drilling in them. The full tree lives in <a href="/guide/skills.html">Skills &amp; Research</a>.</p><h2 id="defense" tabindex="-1">Defense <a class="header-anchor" href="#defense" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Defense&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If a visible enemy prowls within sight of an undefended holding, the game warns you and offers an <strong>emergency muster</strong>: recruits or mercenaries straight onto the walls. The warning has a <strong>Take me there</strong> button that flies the map to the threatened holding, so you can see what is coming before you decide whether to defend it.</p><p>The 🏛 button on the HUD opens the Holdings hub on <strong>All holdings</strong>: every holding with its walls, its garrison and how firmly you hold its lands, followed by your armies in the field. Each row has its own <strong>Take me there</strong>. An undefended gate is flagged in red at the top of the list, so you spot it before an enemy does. Tap a row to open that holding without leaving the hub.</p><p>Each holding&#39;s own <strong>Garrison</strong> section is where the walls are worked: who stands them, who is marching out from them, and, where the holding musters, a Follower or a mercenary raised straight onto them.</p><p>Garrison duty pays on its own, too. A soldier standing a wall earns a slow trickle of <strong>experience every day</strong>: the wall is a drill yard. Heroes learn from deeds, not drills, but a green recruit posted to a quiet hold comes back sharper. Between the drills and their wages, a standing garrison keeps your surplus working instead of piling up.</p>',50)])])}const f=t(h,[["render",l]]);export{w as __pageData,f as default};
